-- #822 S0 REGRESSION PIN (adversarial review of PR #1011).  Declaring a graded
-- interface must not change how ANY other type in the program is elaborated.
--
-- `bad` is ill-typed and has nothing to do with `G`: its `f` is an ordinary type
-- variable that happens to be spelled like `G`'s row-indexed head, at the same
-- arity.  The first cut of #822 keyed graded kinds by the bare parameter name, so
-- `bad`'s `f` adopted `G`'s `[KRow, KType]`; `Int` and `String` both landed in the
-- Row slot, where a non-row argument erases to `<>` (rowArgOf's catch-all), the two
-- arguments became identical, and this program TYPE-CHECKED — exit 0, scheme printed
-- as `bad : a <> b -> a <> b`.
--
-- Delete the interface and the same file is correctly rejected on any build, which is
-- what makes this discriminating: the ONLY thing that can flip it is kind leakage.
interface G (f : Effect -> Type -> Type) where
  g : f e a -> (a -> <e> f e b) -> f e b

bad : f Int a -> f String a
bad x = x
